#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221174 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221412 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "c953f344-77dd-5eaa-9f57-33626a392041" :question/math-academy-id "e-21665" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](images/82/82b6d431638e3abb1c1aebf818f3fbe9f2eb92c7232a3b83c93dc6cb4e563c91.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221175 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/id #uuid "e7405495-e774-5508-b22b-e72d82eb4346" :question/math-academy-id "e-21666" :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\nI. Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\nII. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIII. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\nIV. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1747e7bb-f792-5fd2-924f-7a609818f888" :answer/