97}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "328f9205-a9cf-5f2a-86df-fbc1b8a89ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "equilibrium" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417197} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7318970-d325-5a77-90ca-e5bc8bfab71c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417209} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1115c611-afc1-502d-b2ee-dd7d365f08c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417195} {:answer/id #uuid "e24d08e2-1fa3-5d55-a408-d06d6f1eec89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24d08e2-1fa3-5d55-a408-d06d6f1eec89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417205} :answer-field/id #uuid "f131aa45-b457-59cb-b20c-8d7c35137875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28178800-4a7a-5ca7-893f-5c0dd26ea830" :question/math-academy-id "q-332966" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe a **stable** equilibrium point.\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium\n{{field-2}} \nthe \n{{field-3}} \nas \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium $\\text{but may not converge to}$ the $\\text{equilibrium}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3326532c-6904-5726-8b28-37d12fc59053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305633} {:answer/id #uuid "36132c23-5815-5cc4-8bec-9773d06472ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions approach the equilibrium only along specific trajectories." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305672} {:answer/id #uuid "55a2abaf-34b3-5657-a1de-9ab5577e137e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "