n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddec3d21-d9a8-5b05-8b8b-bf5789fceefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396846} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddafa6a-7da8-507f-81ed-08fd9d2db8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "far from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddec3d21-d9a8-5b05-8b8b-bf5789fceefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396846} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fae1d8c-606a-5bd4-a555-2a631ad7f437" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396845} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3edfa3a9-5202-50e5-bd72-0e10a5d47289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186